<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  Regolith surface for the Lunabotics arena: 9.44 x 8.40 m, the 9.14 x 8.10 m
  bed plus the 0.15 m wall on each side so the terrain tucks under the walls
  with no gap, centred on the bed at (0, 4.05) in the guidebook Origin Point
  frame, with mean grade at world z = 0.

  READ THIS BEFORE MOVING THE TERRAIN.  gz-sim places the two heightmap paths
  from different fields, and neither one alone moves both:

      collision  uses the model <pose>,  IGNORES <pos>
      visual     uses <pos>,             IGNORES the model <pose>

  So the offset has to be written twice - once in the model <pose> for the
  collision, once in the visual's <pos> - and the two must be kept identical.
  Set only one and you get solid ground with nothing drawn on it, or sand you
  drive straight through.  (Verified by dropping bodies onto the collision
  surface and ranging the visual with a downward gpu_lidar at 13 points across
  the bed: the two now agree everywhere.)

  The terrain source also changed.  It used to be dem/moon.tif driven from its
  GeoTIFF elevations, which cannot survive being squeezed to arena scale: those
  elevations are around -212 m, and the collision path rebases to the DEM
  minimum while the visual path keeps the raw value, so the two are 212 m apart
  before any offset is applied at all.

  dem/regolith_heightmap.png is the same terrain shape resampled to 129 x 129
  (gz image heightmaps must be square with a side of 2^k + 1) and normalised to
  0..1.  An image heightmap has no elevation offset for the two paths to
  disagree about - heights simply run from 0 to the third <size> component.

  Relief is 0.30 m rather than the 1 m of the pre-squeeze world, which is a
  regolith bed rather than a dune at 9 m across; the guidebook's craters and
  boulders are the real relief.  The raster's mean normalised height is 0.7067,
  so an offset of -0.212 (= -0.7067 * 0.30) puts mean grade at world z = 0.
  If you change the relief, move both Z offsets with it.

  The diffuse texture is regolith_diffuse.png, derived from moon_diffuse.png by
  renormalising it to a light grey.  sand.jpg is a warm tan photo that visibly
  blotches when tiled at this scale, and moon_diffuse.png tiles cleanly but has
  a mean of 96/255, which renders as near-black under this world's lighting.
  LHS-2E is a light grey highlands simulant, so the albedo is lifted to ~186.

  Note: the 40-50 cm craters of guidebook section 3.1 are NOT modelled.  At
  129 x 129 over 9.44 m the raster is fine enough to carve them in (~7 cm per
  cell) if you want them.
-->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <model name="moon">
    <static>true</static>
    <!-- Positions the COLLISION heightmap only; must match the visual <pos>. -->
    <pose>0 4.05 -0.212 0 0 0</pose>
    <link name="link">
      <collision name="collision">
        <geometry>
          <heightmap>
            <uri>dem/regolith_heightmap.png</uri>
            <size>9.44 8.40 0.30</size>
            <pos>0 0 0</pos> <!-- ignored by the collision path; see above -->
          </heightmap>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <geometry>
          <heightmap>
            <use_terrain_paging>false</use_terrain_paging>
            <texture>
              <diffuse>materials/textures/regolith_diffuse.png</diffuse>
              <normal>materials/textures/moon_normal.png</normal>
              <size>2</size>
            </texture>
            <uri>dem/regolith_heightmap.png</uri>
            <size>9.44 8.40 0.30</size>
            <!-- Positions the VISUAL heightmap only; must match the model <pose>. -->
            <pos>0 4.05 -0.212</pos>
            <sampling>2</sampling> <!-- Sampling rate for the heightmap -->
          </heightmap>
        </geometry>
      </visual>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
